Design content details:

**Rationale.** QuaRot applies Hadamard rotations in a computation-invariant way: for a linear layer y = xWᵀ, inserting HHᵀ (H orthogonal, H symmetric-normalized) gives y = (xH)(HᵀWᵀ) = (xH)(WH)ᵀ. Two distinct insertions:
- Offline (weight-side): rotate weight matrices before quantization — W' = W H (for the second-moment-matched case), quantize W' instead of W; folded into checkpoint, zero runtime cost. For layers where the input-side rotation is not otherwise available (e.g., k/v projections, down_proj input), QuaRot uses "pairwise" Hadamard: quantize W in rotated basis.
- Online (activation-side): multiply activations by H at runtime (R1 full hidden-size Hadamard applied to residual/attention input, R2 per-head Hadamard on V before o_proj, R3 per-intermediate-dim Hadamard on act(SiLU output) before down_proj). Runtime cost O(n log n) per token per application.
In weight-only (A16KV16), activation-side rotations buy nothing for activation quantization error (activations are FP16), so any measured gain must be attributed to (i) weight-side rotation reducing quantization noise in weights and (ii) online rotations changing which weight matrix column-space is rotated via invariance pairing (i.e., they exist to make weight-side rotation applicable to layers that could not otherwise absorb a rotation, e.g. down_proj / o_proj, at the cost of runtime Hadamards). That's the crisp framing the researcher asked for in amendment 1.

So the design must separate: QuaRot-W (offline-only, weight-side rotation applied only where absorbable for free) vs QuaRot-W+O (with online Hadamards to extend rotation coverage to down_proj/o_proj, incurring runtime cost). Report runtime overhead separately.

